messages:  ["Please act as a math teacher and solve the product problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of <<number1 such as 27>> and <<number2 such as 153>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. <<number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.>>\n1. <<Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153>>\n2. <<Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153>>\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.\nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 967 and 692?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's solve this multiplication step-by-step as a math teacher would explain it to a student.\n\n### Problem:\nWhat is the product of 967 and 692?\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\nTo multiply 967 by 692, we will use the standard algorithm for multiplication. This involves multiplying each digit of the second number by each digit of the first number, then adding up the results.\n\n1. **Multiplying by the ones place of 692 (which is 2):**\n   - Multiply 967 by 2:\n     \\[\n     967 \\times 2 = 1934\n     \\]\n\n2. **Multiplying by the tens place of 692 (which is 90):**\n   - Multiply 967 by 90:\n     \\[\n     967 \\times 90 = 87030\n     \\]\n   *Note: Multiplying by 90 is the same as multiplying by 9 and then by 10.*\n\n3. **Multiplying by the hundreds place of 692 (which is 600):**\n   - Multiply 967 by 600:\n     \\[\n     967 \\times 600 = 580200\n     \\]\n   *Note: Multiplying by 600 is the same as multiplying by 6 and then by 100.*\n"]
