messages:  ["Please act as a math teacher and solve the product problem in the given template.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of <<number1 such as 27>> and <<number2 such as 153>>?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. <<number2 153 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.>>\n1. <<Multiply number1 27 by the ones place digit 3 of number2 153>>\n2. <<Multiply number1 27 by the tens place digit 50 of number2 153>>\n<<other digits of number2 if it has>>\nAnswer:\nNow, sum all the step results: <<sum of the results>>.\nSo, the final computed product is <<answer>>.\n####\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 661 and 819?\n# Reasoning:\nLet's solve the problem step by step.\n\n### Problem Statement:\nWhat is the product of 661 and 819?\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\nTo find the product of 661 and 819, we will use long multiplication:\n\n1. **Multiply 661 by the ones place digit (9) of 819:**\n   \\[\n   661 \\times 9 = 5949\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 661 by the tens place digit (10) of 819:**\n   \\[\n   661 \\times 10 = 6610\n   \\]\n   Since 10 is in the tens place, we shift this result one position to the left, effectively multiplying by 10:\n   \\[\n   6610 \\text{ shifted becomes } 66100\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiply 661 by the hundreds place digit (800) of 819:**\n   \\[\n   661 \\times 800 = 528800\n   \\]\n   Since 800 is in the hundreds place, we shift this result two positions to the left, effectively multiplying by 100:\n   \\[\n   528800 \\text{ remains } 528800\n   \\]\n\n### Summing Up All the Partial Products:\n\\[\n5949 + 66100 + 528800 = 600849\n\\]\n"]
